Day 2: Verilog Data Types and Operators
1. Data Types
Data types define the size, representation, and behavior of signals and variables in Verilog.
- wire – Connects signals between gates and modules.
- reg – Stores values assigned in procedural blocks.
- integer – 32-bit signed integer, commonly used in testbenches.
- time – Typically 64-bit unsigned simulation time.
- real / realtime – Floating-point types used mainly in simulation.
- logic – SystemVerilog four-state type commonly used in RTL.
- Four-state values: 0, 1, X (unknown), Z (high impedance).
2. Signed and Unsigned Values
- Unsigned 4-bit: 0 to 15.
- Signed 4-bit: −8 to +7.
- Example: 4'b1111 represents 15 unsigned and −1 signed.
3. Verilog Operators
| Category | Operators | Applications |
|---|---|---|
| Arithmetic | +, -, *, /, % | ALU calculations |
| Relational | <, >, <=, >= | Comparators |
| Equality | ==, !=, ===, !== | Value checking |
| Logical | &&, ||, ! | Condition checking |
| Bitwise | &, |, ^, ~ | Logic gates, parity |
| Reduction | &a, |a, ^a | Checking all bits, parity |
| Shift | <<, >>, <<<, >>> | Bit manipulation |
| Conditional | ?: | Multiplexers |
| Concatenation | {a,b} | Combining signals |
| Replication | {4{1'b1}} | Repeating bit patterns |
| Assignment | =, <= | Combinational and sequential logic |
4. Real-Time Applications
- ALU: Arithmetic and bitwise operators.
- RAM: Vector and array data types.
- FSM: State variables and comparison operators.
- Communication systems: XOR for parity generation and error detection.
- Image processing: 8-bit pixels and arithmetic operations.
- Multiplexer: Conditional operator ?:.
5. Key Takeaway
Understanding data types and operators helps you write efficient RTL code, design digital circuits, debug simulations, and prepare for VLSI interviews.
